    {
        "content": "<p>wareya <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/1598#issuecomment-4101802417\">commented</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/1598\">issue #1598</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Has there been any movement on this? Something like it is a precondition to implementing any mem2reg-like thing safely, and the alternatives proposed so far seem to be research-project-worthy and hard to implement.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 580773458,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1774057846
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>bjorn3 <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/1598#issuecomment-5344426177\">commented</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/1598\">issue #1598</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This has become a fair bit more important now that Cranelift has introduced memory optimizations.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 617482153,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1787153910
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>sunfishcode edited a <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/1598#issuecomment-619389646\">comment</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/1598\">issue #1598</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Giving frontends a way to provide explicit memory dependencies, and using that to implement volatile semantics would be cool. If anyone's interested in a designing a system to do this, it'd be good to start with a design document so we can look at how it'll impact compile times in fast-compilation use cases, and other tradeoffs involved.</p>\n<p>We may want to do something simpler for now that just addresses volatile, and I have a possible alternative. Traditional volatile flags on regular memory accesses turn out to be <a href=\"https://www.cs.utah.edu/~regehr/papers/emsoft08-preprint.pdf\">very error prone</a>. The recommended solution is to transform volatile into a call or separate opcode, and optionally inline the call after all optimizations. It's now how it systematically eliminates a common class of volatile bugs, however it does add complexity in the backend. So instead, how about the following design:</p>\n<p>Add new two instructions to the IR:</p>\n<p>- <code>y = volatile_unary(x)</code> - observe the value of <code>x</code>, do side effects, and return a new value<br>\n  - <code>volatile_rmw(p)</code> - observe the value of <code>*p</code>, do side effects, and then clobber <code>*p</code>.</p>\n<p>At a very late stage, <code>volatile_unary</code> would be translated as if it were an identity operation, and <code>volatile_rmw</code> would be translated as if it were a no-op. With those, frontends could then translate a volatile load like this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">volatile_rmw</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">x0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">load</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">volatile_unary</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x0</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and a volatile store like this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">x0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">volatile_unary</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">store</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x0</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">volatile_rmw</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>That is, we'd use plain load and store instructions, but boxed in with volatile operations, both in control flow and data flow, to prevent any code motion or redundancy elimination. It'll get chatty, especially in the volatile-atomic case, but volatile is rare so it seems like an ok tradeoff. This should let most of the compiler automatically do the right thing, at low cost in overall complexity. And, <code>volatile_rmw</code> and <code>volatile_unary</code> would be handy besides for crafting compiler unit tests :-).</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 617566737,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1787182105
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>TastyCZ <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/1598#issuecomment-6359450688\">commented</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/1598\">issue #1598</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A concrete data point for this. Since #13806 (dead-store elimination) and #14114 (dead <code>notrap</code> loads aren't lowered), a frontend has no flag combination that keeps a volatile access intact. Even plain loads and stores with the default trapping flags get merged:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">function</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">%</span><span class=\"n\">store_twice</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"n\">block0</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">v1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iconst</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">store</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">v2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iconst</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">store</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">function</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">%</span><span class=\"n\">load_twice</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"n\">block0</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">v1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">load</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">v2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">load</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">v3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iadd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v3</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">function</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">%</span><span class=\"n\">store_then_load</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"n\">block0</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">v1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iconst</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">store</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">v2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">load</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v2</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">function</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">%</span><span class=\"n\">unused_notrap</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"n\">block0</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">v1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">load</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">notrap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>clif-util compile -p -D --target x86_64 --set opt_level=speed volatile.clif</code> (main at 65e9dcc3e3) gives:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">function</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">%</span><span class=\"n\">store_twice</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fast</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"n\">block0</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">v2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iconst</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">store</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">function</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">%</span><span class=\"n\">load_twice</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">fast</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"n\">block0</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">v1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">load</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">v3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iadd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v3</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">function</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">%</span><span class=\"n\">store_then_load</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">fast</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"n\">block0</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">i64</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">v1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">iconst</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"kt\">i32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">store</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v0</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v1</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>%unused_notrap</code> compiles to an empty body, even at <code>opt_level=none</code>.</p>\n<p>Each of these is correct for ordinary memory. For MMIO or other memory with side effects they aren't, and the frontend has nothing to say otherwise. rustc_codegen_cranelift is affected: <code>read_volatile</code>/<code>write_volatile</code> are merged, forwarded and dropped at <code>-Copt-level&gt;=1</code> (<a href=\"https://github.com/rust-lang/rustc_codegen_cranelift/issues/1715\">rust-lang/rustc_codegen_cranelift#1715</a>).</p>\n<p>We implemented the simple version of this issue: a <code>volatile</code> MemFlag (text format <code>volatile</code>) that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>inst_predicates:</strong> a volatile load has a side effect, so it is lowered even when unused and is never pure for the egraph (no GVN, LICM or rematerialization).</li>\n<li><strong>alias analysis:</strong> a volatile access is never replaced by a known value, never removed as a redundant or dead store, never the source of a forwarded value, and never makes another store dead. It acts as a barrier for what is known about memory.</li>\n<li><strong>lowering:</strong> a volatile load is never sunk into its user's memory operand, so its count and width stay as written.</li>\n<li><strong>verifier:</strong> rejects <code>volatile</code> together with <code>readonly</code> or <code>can_move</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It comes with filetests for alias analysis, the egraph, the verifier and load sinking on x64 and aarch64. Accesses without the flag are unaffected, so it costs nothing for code that doesn't use it.</p>\n<p>The <code>volatile_unary</code>/<code>volatile_rmw</code> design suggested above would also work. The flag turned out small: about 130 lines outside the tests, mostly in alias analysis, which is now where memory accesses get merged. If a MemFlag is acceptable, I'm happy to open a PR.<br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>",
